ction/27999668ae54f1bce0dd8d35362aaff749aa9e83b32413608fd1de53c20aeb43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82476" :question/id #uuid "fe613ef8-04a3-5d79-9265-7f217d762c45" :question/math-academy-id "q-82476" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -11\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -24 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -24 \\\\ 5 & -11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82476/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection/ac22547a1e6aa30369d11240fabc2e206de0be8bdb7cc402ea892776b65947c0" :answer/id #uuid "f465c367-90b2-58e4-a879-d5ba382a8565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -13 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection/8f8ec89fc4c9c93ef989995df79beae005ccdf0a1fa8b0c79a99aca2a52b1fa8" :answer/id #uuid "44f68f07-01da-5631-937b-549aab9ced85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-23 & -26 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection/29c1fa0f1222c3294813ccffc37d3987da51e96babfcca3ea6f531f0b40db0fb" :answer/id #uuid "03893ac9-fb74-5ac9-994e-4cac252d42e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -23 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection/ed7d9c95cafe5ff8b3963d024f64dbd03f16422905126f86064aa3fd0f35ced6" :answer/id #uuid "f8f1cac3-7405-5578-af08-c6890ccebbd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -23 \\\\ 10 & -26\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection/e75eeb8cad10ff2842514fac4269345aebe41d55437ed13728b5ba256fbf3aa5" :answer/id #uuid "48f95872-3d6f-5372-a370-6b3493822e78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -23 \\\\ 8 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d740ebf-dc1a-504a-87fa-558f52762bc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38191/selection/29c1fa0f1222c3294813ccffc37d3987da51e96babfcca3ea6f531f0b40db0fb" :answer-field/choices ["q-38191/selection/ac22547a1e6aa30369d11240fabc2e206de0be8bdb7cc402ea892776b65947c0" "q-38191/selection/8f8ec89fc4c9c93ef989995df79beae005ccdf0a1fa8b0c79a99aca2a52b1fa8" "q-38191/selection/29c1fa0f1222c3294813ccffc37d3987da51e96babfcca3ea6f531f0b40db0fb" "q-38191/selection/ed7d9c95cafe5ff8b3963d024f64dbd03f16422905126f86064aa3fd0f35ced6" "q-38191/selection/e75eeb8cad10ff2842514fac4269345aebe41d55437ed13728b5ba256fbf3aa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38191" :question/id #uuid "96d9426d-d53e-5099-931a-e00fd3ad762a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38191" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -8 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -26 & -23 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -23 \\\\ 10 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38191/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38191"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38188"] :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "da22e684-574c-5597-8892-fc7c4d2f97cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "682daa3a-47f3-5d4e-a3d7-e001d5b60911" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "518a7797-c501-5141-8bc9-b067152b8264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f9b04806-07c5-5ed1-8bbf-af3ec7b7a4d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ae879c84-cdac-5172-8dbf-0751cfc391fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "334af050-066b-5133-9780-0f4a17b516c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140370/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-140370/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-140370/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-140370/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-140370/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-140370/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140370" :question/id #uuid "6bff5b79-c4c4-59cb-ac72-ccf6e3b94c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-140370" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & b & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -4 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 0 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 16 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -4 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{2} & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 16 & 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -4 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{3}{2} & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -4 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=0,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+0+2=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140370/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140370"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6bfefc0c-9154-5bb9-85c8-2bef3e182a05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "bfa38bb6-8bd5-5b50-9258-e4098d388270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "f96a556d-37f3-5be2-a782-3782f3aff174" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "56b76242-68f8-53c1-8113-36563aea659a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "399250bd-50f8-592f-b173-52c89cd48e9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d500a7c-34e1-50f7-814f-ba10398000e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82493/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-82493/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-82493/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-82493/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-82493/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-82493/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82493" :question/id #uuid "901a7752-63f9-595e-b3ca-5fde31aa9817" :question/math-academy-id "q-82493" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & c \\\\ a & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & b & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=0,$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+0+1=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82493/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82493"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "8b66412a-d50c-545a-82e7-fde6c4c6a243" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "07153153-1545-5caf-8142-3a2c278861c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "61800857-3368-595d-89cd-21010956abff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "4f68aa3e-b461-55b2-9285-571e2216e060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection/59cabed12271767d3647412725d382bc72464274dc4ac46b4e7903c969156a02" :answer/id #uuid "059e8efa-cbbf-53e4-96f9-edd1a729a993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "29a3c513-11e7-5b00-b9c0-1925b42e2aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82501/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer-field/choices ["q-82501/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-82501/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-82501/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-82501/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" "q-82501/selection/59cabed12271767d3647412725d382bc72464274dc4ac46b4e7903c969156a02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82501" :question/id #uuid "d5b74837-c061-5175-ab3e-c37b5522a248" :question/math-academy-id "q-82501" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 & c \\\\ -1 & b & -3 \\\\ a & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y-x \\\\ y+z-x\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 & -2 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-3,$ $b=1,$ $c=19$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+1+19=17.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82501/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82501"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "c42e8cff-06c6-5f18-b834-1ea48a56f530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "6228fe05-8d98-55a8-9d30-aa45a2bec386" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8f26fe73-7492-5c31-9114-512e4affaadb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "a897ae1a-6a9a-5661-9d54-205d2078f611" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "c340e658-5cec-53fa-8ec6-f684f98806d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17cf39ae-1f6c-5bcb-9e53-145cf4c03b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82497/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-82497/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-82497/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-82497/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-82497/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-82497/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82497" :question/id #uuid "7443a7d1-d654-500f-8538-14300634b4d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82497" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & b & -25 \\\\ a & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}y-z \\\\ x+y-z \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 & 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=3,$ $c=7$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+3+7=10.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82497/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82497"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "52c94636-c473-5bf3-a2e5-916b71809f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "396045b8-ca1c-5422-8716-1a9e94eda4f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ba1c3627-f331-582c-a096-4e538344aabb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "4bdf0938-d0f5-571d-abc6-501c7ed909b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "21386267-5ce4-5b34-8f4a-49a1b02d1135" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "15674e07-55fb-5b26-84c4-6d91338ffc04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82483/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-82483/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-82483/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-82483/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-82483/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-82483/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82483" :question/id #uuid "b2611bb2-69cb-59a6-b47c-190ed89de3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82483" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & b & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2})=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82483/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82483"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5514"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/ce5c4a3b67cdbcb3fd7a808d201cc5dd0247408788957487d6b6898622cd0ff8.png)" :question/worked-solution "The image of $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7249"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/e07cc9382ff64be2d3ad03974dee5e61f814457f06dbe4e755a72e69efc7608c.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/9b7de12d5e69074e64281b8060088e458c04007ed48f7640e85619898f96dad7.png)\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/2dd5d507303f356fcd3632c8dade2e8a656169de1118b4443bd651cca45d6660.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ $c=0$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5513"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/ec340f6251c31a0445c5e3db9e23488e783e36eb2cc2e3f85d83993a3ac87f79.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/5228bf96b160625889e18bd57146d6ce092f48859f5a96bd77e4832a0fc6362f.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/32aab8b678ff195f1ce2edf43bd1565890328a8926aa22c31b1850936e54a927.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5515"] :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/6efd50facaf6c5a4d4b536eaa194bbfcf240cf30804bb13a7a6ac479adf6d864.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/1d585e7b839d1e569693d7489c23e47761e2f96bdf114ee96f4b287aa68d7fa4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/318cfa577ad284a0b606eb58232ee0276deedcbd446357e7f5b4c690b46b2208.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/5181a3a65f33553fad4c1ebf5ac81e9c2e3957576ac0501b953afcbf8c09349d.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-2)+(-2) = -3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
